{
	title: 'Web Tab indents where indentation fits',
	spec: `Web message box, refining task 9x: Tab indents only where indentation is plausible, and moves focus elsewhere, so the box never traps the keyboard (WCAG 2.1.2).

Tab, with no Ctrl, Alt or Cmd, inserts a literal tab when the caret, with no selection, sits in the leading whitespace of any line but the first (at column 0 or after only spaces and tabs), and indents as in task 9x when a selection spans lines. Shift-Tab removes one level as in task 9x when a selection spans lines or the caret sits in the leading whitespace of an indented line. Slash completion keeps priority. Anywhere else (an empty box, the first line, after text on a line, a selection within one line) Tab and Shift-Tab move focus natively.

Escape hatch: moving the caret with arrow keys, End, or Cmd-Up to a point after text or onto the first line makes the next Tab move focus. Escape is not the hatch (unlike CodeMirror) because it pauses a running turn. The terminal is unchanged.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Research: CodeMirror leaves Tab unbound by default for WCAG 2.1.2; with indentWithTab, Escape then Tab moves focus, and Ctrl-M (Shift-Alt-M on macOS) toggles tab focus mode. VS Code offers Ctrl-M "Toggle Tab Key Moves Focus" and never traps Tab in read-only files. Hal uses caret context instead because Escape pauses turns.'
	],
	needs: [],
	foldInto: ['9x']
}
